#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162757} :activity/id #uuid "50fb9a01-de8e-5ba2-ab6c-78aa8023faaf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050018 :topic/id #uuid "262e93f1-d6a9-53d7-b9fd-58feb52cc268" :topic/math-academy-id 3074} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163182 :tutorial/content "A continuous random variable $X$ follows an **exponential distribution** if it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{λe^{- λx},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThe **rate parameter** $\\lambda$ is a positive real number, and\n\n$$\ne\\approx 2.71828\n$$\n\nis Euler's number.\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows an exponential distribution, we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)\n$$\n\nThe exponential distribution models the amount of continuous space or time between two events. In a future lesson, we will learn how to model situations using the exponential distribution. But for now, let's get some practice working with the PDF to compute probabilities." :tutorial/id #uuid "c2a50890-7334-508e-bac0-19d71ccce3db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10784 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7a402afc-6129-5cac-b4c5-dce09e83e3f7" :step/math-academy-id 224790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162758}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161930 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161745 :question/id #uuid "6d3ad56f-a242-5335-aa48-e2608b1b973d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16584"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cc5827c-739e-52fd-9f10-a75bf2c18c22" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162685}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a \"Less Than\" Probability With the Exponential Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "57a0fac0-7935-51d2-8db4-40cd4f4ff7e2" :step/math-academy-id 224791 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162759}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161931 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161746 :question/id #uuid "f1adfc5c-78aa-5f14-bec5-cf763d86e5af" :question/math-academy-id "e-16585"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1677dd6-74e4-5778-bbba-fbb230c7646a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162683}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a \"Greater Than\" Probability With the Exponential Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "aaebd855-e8da-5148-bcca-168a8644d4fd" :step/math-academy-id 224794 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162760}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161932 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161747 :question/id #uuid "fffe6dba-c0a1-5644-a975-6d51b6de2f98" :question/math-academy-id "e-16586"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c89beba-5605-514e-82c3-8e8145b178c9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162687}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on a Bounded Interval Using the Exponential Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "32e9215c-bd3d-5527-bbf4-9e6f37a98e5f" :step/math-academy-id 224797 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162761}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163183 :tutorial/content "Consider a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$, and two positive real numbers $s, t$.\n\nSuppose we're given that $X>s$, and we want to determine the probability that $X>s+t$. In other words, we wish to compute the conditional probability\n$P(X>s+t \\,\\big \\mid \\, X>s)$.\n\nBy the definition of conditional probability, we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>s) = \\dfrac{P\\big((X> s+t) \\cap (X > s)\\big)}{P(X>s)}\n$$\n\nNotice that $X>s+t$ implies that $X>s$, therefore\n\n$$\nP\\big((X> s+t) \\cap (X > s)\\big) = P(X > s+t)\n$$\n\nSo, the above conditional probability simplifies to\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\,\\big \\mid \\, X>s) = \\dfrac{P(X> s+t)}{P(X>s)}\n$$\n\nNext, let's recall how to calculate $P(X > a)$ for some $a>0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > a) &= 1 - P(X < a) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\int_{0}^{a}λe^{- λx}dx \\\\\n&= 1 + \\left.e^{- λx}\\right|_{0}^{a} \\\\\n&= 1 + e^{- λa} - e^{0} \\\\\n&= e^{- λa}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can use this fact to calculate $P(X> s+t)$ and $P(X>s)$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) &= (P(X > s + t))/(P(X > s)) \\\\\n&= (e^{- λ(s + t)})/(e^{- λs}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{- λs} \\cdot e^{- λt}}{e^{- λs}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{- λs} \\cdot e^{- λt}}{e^{- λs}} \\\\\n&= e^{- λt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that\n\n$$\ne^{-\\lambda t} = P(X > t)\n$$\n\nSo, we have proved the following property:\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\big \\mid X>s) = P(X > t)\n$$\n\nThis property is called **memoryless property.** It can be proved that the exponential distribution is the only continuous distribution with this property (note that the geometric distribution is the only *discrete* distribution with the memoryless property).\n\nIntuitively, the memoryless property states that the probability of an event occurring after a certain amount of time has already elapsed is independent of how much time has passed. This means that the distribution \"forgets\" what has happened before time $s$.\n\nWe'll discuss how the memoryless property is used for mathematical modeling in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "02202f83-1511-550a-ab05-645cfdda4003" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10785 :tutorial/title "The Memoryless Property"} :step/id #uuid "2894872e-ce40-5e6f-a5b3-1261d99d3e24" :step/math-academy-id 224800 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162762}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163185 :tutorial/content "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(2)$, let's calculate the following probability:\n\n$$\nP\\left(X > \\dfrac32 \\,\\bigg \\mid \\, X > \\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nGiven a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ and two positive real numbers $s, t$, the memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 2, \\qquad s=\\dfrac 12,\\qquad s+t=\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can find $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt = (t+s) - s = \\dfrac32-\\dfrac12 = 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > \\frac{3}{2} \\mid X > \\frac{1}{2}) &= e^{- λt} \\\\\n&= e^{- (2) \\cdot (1)} \\\\\n&= e^{-2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{e^{2}} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.135\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "91736de3-87f0-59ed-839b-e2d12dc5744d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10800 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "8a4bba0d-64ae-53f7-b86a-7458bdc9351d" :step/math-academy-id 224801 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161933 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161748 :question/id #uuid "88232e58-999e-5ac8-8f09-8a4b204ec51c" :question/math-academy-id "e-16587"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aab06631-cd2c-57d2-9518-25f7a31c4b6b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050108}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162534}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property"} :step/id #uuid "9a238cab-5690-5d48-8149-4d2eee2010b3" :step/math-academy-id 224802}] :activity/title "The Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162203}]}
